Pennsylvania judge handed out the first  jail sentences in the hazing death of  Penn State sophomore Timothy Piazza the  19 year old died in February of 2017  after an alcohol fueled event at the  Beta Theta Pi fraternity house Piazza's  death led to criminal charges against 28  former fraternity members three of them  will now serve time behind bars  Jericka Duncan is here with the details  of their punishment  Jericka good morning good morning we've  been covering this story from the very  very beginning this has been one of the  largest hazing prosecutions in US  history the three former Beta Theta Pi  brothers who received jail time  yesterday had pleaded guilty to charges  related to hazing 21-year old Luke  Visser and Michael Bonaduce were both  sentenced to up to six months in jail 20  year-old Joshua Carducci received up to  nine months the judge also gave 21  year-old Joseph Saleh up to 10 months of  house arrest all four men also received  probation and will pay fines this urbana  Tucci and Saleh were given community  service the judge may later amend the  three sentences to house arrest  Piazza family attorney Tom Klein said  Timothy's parents Jim and Evelyn were in  the courtroom for yesterday's sentencing  Klein said it was a significant step  forward in the long road to justice  meanwhile there are still more members  to be sentenced last year a judge  dismissed the most serious allegations  against some of the students including  felony aggravated assault charges  a judge also tossed involuntary  manslaughter charges against the  fraternities president and pledge master  who are still awaiting trial and I can  tell you speaking with those parents  when all of this first happened they  were determined to see this thing  through and also not just because they  wanted justice and reference to the  people they believed were involved but  they also want to make sure that message  of how dangerous hazing is gets out  there they want to prevent standing on  their feet every time I see that picture  of Piazza in the football jersey or with  this baseball cap turned on backwards  again and we've all talked to his  parents and you feel their pain and they  really do want to help others but they  also want justice for their son I hope  this gives them some amount of comfort  because nothing will bring him back we  know that  Tajima Neverland I think of them all the  time I do - nor I think of them and they  are doing is so admirable letting it be  known that there will be punishment for  this kind of behavior they are trying to  will form this fraternity system  absolutely thank you drink a you've been  on this room there they were in court  again yesterday imagine that nor you  have to sit listen to this again
 

